From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:45:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Evict a cached page whose connection gets a
 versionchange event

A document in the back/forward cache is not fully active, so the event
loop holds back its tasks until it is shown again. A versionchange
event for one of its IndexedDB connections waited there, and with it
the upgrade or delete that fired it, which ran only once the user went
back to the page, or never (back-forward-cache-open-connection timed
out).

Like Gecko and Chromium, evict the page instead: the connection result
channel of a window hands versionchange events to a task that runs even
while the document is not fully active. If the document isn't fully
active, the task asks the constellation to evict it with a message of
its own, EvictFromBackForwardCache. The constellation walks from the
document's pipeline up to the first pipeline that isn't the current one
of its browsing context, which is the pipeline of the cached session
history entry also for a cross-site iframe in another script thread,
closes it, and reloads the entry if it is traversed to again; it does
nothing if the document is fully active again because the user went
back. Either way the task then queues the event as a normal task, behind
the tasks held back so far, such as the open request's success that
creates the connection: the event loop drops it with an evicted
document, and it fires as before in a fully active one. Closing the
pipeline runs the window's IndexedDB teardown, and the backend counts a
connection of a gone global as closed, so the upgrade or delete goes on.
The IndexedDB specification doesn't cover this case. Pages with open
connections stay eligible for the cache; only a versionchange evicts
them. Worker globals are unaffected.

The P5 review changed the first version of this commit (S3 and S4): it
made the topmost inactive document of its own script thread
unsalvageable and sent DiscardDocument, which closed only a cross-site
iframe, so its cached parent was restored with the iframe closed, and
could discard a page the user had just gone back to; and it fired the
event inline, which could overtake the success that creates the
connection and was then dropped. The fork test gained a cross-site
iframe.

Validation: build host SM and QuickJS release and run IndexedDB/back-forward-cache-open-connection.window.html, back-forward-cache-open-transaction.window.html and the new /_mozilla/IndexedDB/back-forward-cache-versionchange.window.html (a delete and an upgrade evict the cached page, also for a connection of its iframe; a cached page with a connection is still restored) with --repeat 3: all pass on both engines, while the previous commit's binary times out on both connection tests; host IndexedDB/ 4,484 of 4,511 on SM and on QuickJS with the new pass as the only change, /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 312 of 312 SM, 310 of 312 QuickJS (interface lists, as before); /workers/ and html/browsers/browsing-the-web/back-forward-cache/ without unexpected results on both engines; after the P5 review fixes: the fork test has a fourth subtest, a cross-site iframe (HTTP_NOTSAMESITE_ORIGIN) whose connection gets the versionchange; it and the same-origin iframe subtest fail on a binary whose constellation discards the reporting pipeline itself as DiscardDocument did (P5.3/review-fixes/bfc-neg.raw), and all four pass on the stack tip, as do back-forward-cache-open-connection and back-forward-cache-open-transaction; host SM release builds, 151 passed, 1 skipped at this commit
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent found where the test hung, implemented the eviction, wrote the fork test and measured it on both engines; the P5 review fixes added the constellation eviction message, queued the event behind held tasks, added the cross-site iframe subtest and ran a negative control)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
